\description{
Seeds the global random state, analogous to base R's \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}}. Calling
it is optional: until the global random state is first drawn from, it is not
set, and the first draw derives it from base R's RNG, so a \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}}
before that makes the draws reproducible too.

All random draws in anvl -- the samplers \code{\link[=nv_runif]{nv_runif()}}, \code{\link[=nv_rnorm]{nv_rnorm()}},
\code{\link[=nv_rbinom]{nv_rbinom()}}, \code{\link[=nv_sample_int]{nv_sample_int()}} and \code{\link[=nv_sample]{nv_sample()}}, and the primitive
\code{\link[=prim_sample_bits]{prim_sample_bits()}} they build on -- draw from the global random state. It
is a key and a counter of the ThreeFry-2x32 generator. Every call of
\code{\link[=prim_sample_bits]{prim_sample_bits()}}, a \emph{sampling call}, splits it once: it draws from a
fresh key derived from the key and the counter, and advances the counter by
one, however many values it drew. A sampler advances the counter by its
number of sampling calls: \code{\link[=nv_rnorm]{nv_rnorm()}} by two, the others by one.

A \code{\link[=jit]{jit()}}-compiled function makes the same sampling calls as the same code
run without \code{\link[=jit]{jit()}}, in the same order, so it draws the same values: the
compiled program takes the global random state as an input, and after the
call returns, the global random state is advanced by the number of sampling
calls the program makes, which is known when the function is traced.

A draw in a loop body gives a new sample on every iteration. How \code{\link[=nv_scan]{nv_scan()}},
\code{\link[=nv_if]{nv_if()}} and \code{\link[=nv_while]{nv_while()}} advance the global random state, so that the
number of sampling calls of a program is known when it is traced, is
explained in the vignette "Random Numbers". Sampling is not possible in the
condition of \code{\link[=nv_while]{nv_while()}} or in the functions of a reduction or a scatter.
}
\section{Seeding from base R}{

When the global random state is not set, the next draw derives it from base
R's RNG, consuming two of its uniform draws. This happens only once: after
that, the global random state is advanced by anvl's draws alone, and
\strong{calling \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} again does not reseed it} -- call \code{nv_set_seed()} for
that. \code{nv_set_seed(NULL)} unsets the global random state, so that the next
draw derives it from base R's RNG again.

\code{local_nv_seed()} and \code{with_nv_seed()} seed the global random state for the
calling scope or for \code{code} only, like \code{\link[withr:local_seed]{withr::local_seed()}} and
\code{\link[withr:with_seed]{withr::with_seed()}} do for base R's RNG: the global random state from
before is restored afterwards.

The global random state is seeded when R runs the call, so these functions
cannot be called in a function traced by \code{\link[=jit]{jit()}}, where they would seed it
once, while tracing, rather than on every call.
}

\section{Saving and restoring the random state}{

A seed restarts a sequence from its beginning. To continue one where it
stopped, e.g. when resuming a model fit, save the random state itself:
\code{nv_get_random_state()} returns the global random state, and
\code{nv_set_random_state()} makes a saved one the global random state again. A
random state is an \code{AnvlRandomState}: plain R values, the key and the
counter, each a 64-bit unsigned integer stored as its two 32-bit words, so
\code{\link[=saveRDS]{saveRDS()}} can write it to a file. \code{NULL} is the random state that is not
set. The counter wraps around at \eqn{2^{64}}, where its draws repeat, so
restore a random state that \code{nv_get_random_state()} returned rather than one
made up.
}

\section{Devices}{

The global random state is held by R and copied to the device of each call
that draws from it, and the generator is built from integer arithmetic, so a
seed gives the same random bits whatever devices the draws run on. The
samples computed from them, e.g. by \code{\link[=nv_rnorm]{nv_rnorm()}}, can differ in the last
digits between platforms, as their floating point functions do.
}

